  "_note": "The core body, and nothing on top of it: no helmet, no kit, no faction colour (BUILD-ORDER C2). 1.8 m tall — 18 modules, ART-BIBLE §2's own figure — and 15 parts, which is that table's soldier standard. A pack dresses this; core owns the skeleton and the way it moves.",
  "_bent_by_rest_angles": "Every bone here is authored pointing straight down its own -Z with a whole-module `offset`, and the legs are bent by `rest` angles on their joints rather than by the geometry. That is not a style choice, it is the only thing that works: `offset` must be a whole number of modules and a bone pivots half its own length along its own axis, so a bone rotated by anything that is not a multiple of 90° puts its joint at an irrational position and there is no legal `offset` that reconnects the chain to it. `rest` is plain degrees on a joint and is constrained by nothing, and `Rig.build` applies it — so the rest pose comes out bent while the file stays on the grid. The fixtures under `tests/fixtures/rig/` do it the other way, with a small offset jog, because their whole point is that every number can be checked with a pencil; see DEVIATIONS-C2 B5.",
  "_derived": "Read with `--rig core:soldier` rather than worked out here. The numbers that matter — where the sole ends up, how far the body can drop, which way each knee bends — are all consequences of the rest angles below, and the report is what they were tuned against.",
